/target
**/*.rs.bk

# Isolated checkouts for feature work (git worktrees). Git records these in
# .git/worktrees; the checkout itself must never be committed.
/.worktrees/

# Cargo.lock is committed: this workspace ships binaries, and a lockfile is
# what makes a clone build the same dependency graph as CI.

# Local state from running the binaries in the checkout.
/botroster-data
*.log
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Tauri regenerates these at build time.
crates/botroster-app/gen/

# The staged sidecar is a build artefact: `scripts/sidecar.sh` builds it for
# whatever triple is being compiled, and Tauri's build script wants it to exist
# before `botroster-app` will compile. CLAUDE.md says so and it used to be
# ignored, but a 14.4 MB Windows binary was committed and stayed committed,
# which is how it came to be in three consecutive handovers as an open question.
#
# It was not harmless. `ci.yml`'s bundle job greps the staged runtime for the
# builtin signing key, and on Linux and macOS this directory was not empty at
# checkout, so a `find | head -1` returned the committed Windows binary - built
# on a laptop long before that key existed - and reported a correct build as
# broken. The job now names the file it means, and this rule means there is only
# ever one file there to name.
crates/botroster-app/binaries/

# The overnight UX loop. Competitor reference captures and iteration shots stay
# local: origin is public, and this project's position is that it contains none
# of their material. The loop's text artefacts are committed; its images are not.
.claude/ux-loop/reference/
.claude/ux-loop/shots/
.claude/ux-loop/node_modules/
.claude/ux-loop/.baseline-bytes
.claude/ux-loop/.baseline-gzip-bytes

# Generated by scripts/review.sh — logs and scan output, not review artefacts.
.claude/product-review/data/
